# Line-ending policy.
#
# This file exists because of a REAL defect, not as boilerplate.
#
# `.agent/verification/atomic-security-sources.tar.gz.b64` was committed with CRLF
# line endings while the repository had no .gitattributes and core.autocrlf=true.
# `base64 -d` then failed with "base64: invalid input" on every platform, because the
# committed git blob itself carried CR. That silently blocked materialising the three
# security source files and the 15 reconstructed Blockchain test bodies that
# HARNESS_LAWS.md counts toward the 484-test accounting.
#
# The payload was never corrupt: with CR stripped it decodes to a valid gzip stream
# and all three SHA-256 sums verify. The fix is to stop git from rewriting line
# endings in the files where it matters.

# Base64 payloads are binary blobs. Never convert them.
*.b64 -text

# Shell scripts MUST be LF. A CRLF shebang line breaks `sh`, and `set -eu\r` is not
# `set -eu` — the gate would fail with a confusing error rather than running.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Source and data files are LF for the same reason: byte-stable hashing and
# reproducible builds (DOD-002) depend on content that does not change per platform.
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf

# SQL migrations MUST be LF, and this is not cosmetic: `schema_migration` stores a checksum
# of each applied migration (MIG-5), so a CRLF rewrite on another machine makes an unchanged
# migration register as drift. Measured: the committed blobs for 0001–0008 are LF, and this
# rule keeps them that way instead of relying on each contributor's core.autocrlf.
*.sql   text eol=lf

# Archive and image formats are binary by definition.
*.tar.gz binary
*.tgz    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.webp   binary
